{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Is it to YHWH that you would requite this, O foolish and unwise people.</b> The linking of these verses with the preceding verses appears to me along the lines of what is said (Job 35:6–7): ״אִם חָטָאתָ מַה תִּפְעָל בּוֹ וְרַבּוּ פְשָׁעֶיךָ מַה תַּעֲשֶׂה לּוֹ, אִם צָדַקְתָּ מַה תִּתֶּן לוֹ אוֹ מַה מִיָּדְךָ יִקָּח״ — the same order that is mentioned there is mentioned here as well. For before entering with them into words of reproof, he laid down this preface for them: that they should not think anything distorted about HaShem, saying that there is any gain to HaShem, blessed be He, in the matter — whether you do evil or good — and that you might think to say that HaShem, blessed be He, seeks His own advantage, to push away from Himself the harm that reaches Him from your transgressions, and to draw near to Himself the benefit that comes to Him from the fulfillment of the commandments. Regarding this He said: you are mistaken in both. For concerning the matter of corruption He first said: ״שִׁחֵת לוֹ לֹא״, as its Targum renders: ״חַבִּילוּ לְהוֹן וְלֹא לֵיהּ״ — that is to say, ״רַבּוּ פְשָׁעֶיךָ מַה תַּעֲשֶׂה לּוֹ״. And the substance of the corruption is theirs and its blemish is theirs, not that of HaShem, blessed be He. And afterward He spoke concerning the fulfillment of the commandments — for the term גְּמוּל (requital) applies to some good requital — and said: ״הַלְה׳ תִּגְמְלוּ זֹאת״. The letter ה of ״הַלְה׳״ is lengthened, to allude to the five books of the Torah: do you think to requite the Holy One, blessed be He, with some benefit through your fulfilling the five books of the Torah, which are called ״זֹאת״, as it is said (Deuteronomy 4:44): ״וְזֹאת הַתּוֹרָה״?" "<b>O foolish and unwise people.</b> Even though ״וְלֹא חָכָם״ (and unwise) is not the opposite of ״עַם נָבָל״ (foolish people), nevertheless it appears to me that this is its meaning. For the word ״נָבָל״ carries different interpretations among the commentators, and I say that whoever lacks a generous heart is called ״נָבָל״ — proof from what is said (Isaiah 32:5): ״לֹא יִקָּרֵא עוֹד לְנָבָל נָדִיב וּלְכִילַי לֹא יֵאָמֵר שׁוֹעַ״, from which it follows that the נָבָל is the opposite of the נָדִיב (generous one). And Nabal the Carmelite is proof, for he said (I Samuel 25:11): ״וְלָקַחְתִּי אֶת לַחְמִי וְגוֹ׳ וְנָתַתִּי לַאֲנָשִׁים אֲשֶׁר לֹא יָדַעְתִּי אֵי מִזֶּה הֵמָּה״, and concerning him it is said (ibid. 25:25): ״כִּי כִשְׁמוֹ כֶּן הוּא, נָבָל שְׁמוֹ וּנְבָלָה עִמּוֹ״. And perhaps this name is derived from the root of יוּבַל and יְבוּלָהּ — its meaning being: even what you bring in (מוֹבִיל), so too this one who lacks a generous heart — all day long he brings in from others to himself, but he will give nothing at all to others. Now this mistaken person thinks that HaShem, blessed be He, has some need in His commandments. What brought him to think this corrupted thought? It is only because he himself is a נָבָל and not generous, and it is far from his understanding that one person would do some kindness to another for free unless he knows that the recipient of his kindness will return and repay him for his benefit. For if he does not expect repayment — why would he do kindness to another for free? So too does he think concerning the Holy One, blessed be He: when he sees the magnitude of the kindnesses that YHWH has done with him, he thinks — why does He do these kindnesses with me for free? It is only in order that He too may receive from me more benefit than them. Therefore he called one who thinks this thought ״נָבָל״ and not נָדִיב, for it is far from his understanding that HaShem would do kindnesses to him in the manner of generosity and grace. Therefore it is said immediately afterward: ״וַיְנַבֵּל צוּר יְשֻׁעָתוֹ״ — that is to say, he spoke of Him as one speaks of one of the נְבָלִים, and therefore measure for measure: ״בְּגוֹי נָבָל אַכְעִיסֵם״. He further called him ״לֹא חָכָם״ — for besides being a נָבָל and not generous, behold he is a son who is not wise, for not from wisdom did he ask about this." "And afterward he explained, item by item, first thing corresponding to first and last to last. For what he said — ״עַם נָבָל״ — concerning this he said: ״הֲלֹא הוּא אָבִיךָ קָּנֶךָ״. For were it granted — let it be according to your words, that no person does kindness to another for free — these words apply to a stranger. But a father to his son — even one who is stingy toward others — nevertheless, is it not the way of the world that a father does many kindnesses for his son from the day of his birth, in the manner of generosity, for free, and does not expect repayment? So too the Holy One, blessed be He: ״הֲלֹא הוּא אָבִיךָ קָּנֶךָ״ — and He has compassion on you as a father has compassion on children, even though He has no benefit whatsoever from you. And concerning what He called him a son who is not wise — He wanted to prove this by saying: ״הוּא עָשְׂךָ וַיְכֹנְנֶךָ״. Since He made you, if so, whatever you are capable of doing — clearly He, blessed be He, can also do it, and all the more so (קַל וָחוֹמֶר). For if He made you, then whatever you find within your power to do, it is obvious that it is also within His power, blessed be He, to do, as it is said (Psalms 94:9): ״הֲנֹטַע אֹזֶן הֲלֹא יִשְׁמָע, הֲיֹצֵר עַיִן הֲלֹא יַבִּיט״. And as it is said (ibid. 50:12): ״אִם אֶרְעַב לֹא אֹמַר לָךְ, כִּי לִי תֵבֵל וּמְלוֹאָהּ״. Therefore He said: ״הוּא עָשְׂךָ״ — and behold He made you and all that you find within your power to do together, and if so, He, blessed be He, could have done all that you do if He had some need of it. And since you did not understand this thing with your intellect, surely you are a son who is not wise." "And afterward He said: ״זְכוֹר יְמוֹת עוֹלָם״. You, the נָבָל and not generous, for whom it is far-fetched that the Holy One, blessed be He, did a thing in the manner of generosity without expecting repayment — ״זְכוֹר יְמוֹת עוֹלָם״, the days in which the Holy One, blessed be He, created the world. For through this you will be compelled to admit on all accounts that ״עוֹלָם חֶסֶד יִבָּנֶה״ — for behold, before the world was created, He, blessed be He, was alone, and His existence sufficed for itself, and He had no need for any existent thing, for there was not yet anything in existence. And if so, in the days of eternity, when He created His world — surely He created it only from the voluntary giving of His heart and His will, as it is written (Psalms 89:3): ״עוֹלָם חֶסֶד יִבָּנֶה״. ״בִּינוּ שְׁנוֹת דּוֹר וָדוֹר״ — Rashi explained: the generation of the Flood and the generation of the Dispersion. For in these two generations the deeds of all who came into the world were corrupted. And through this He wanted to establish what He had said, ״הוּא עָשְׂךָ וַיְכֹנְנֶךָ״ — for had He some need in your deeds, how could the Holy One, blessed be He, have acted in these two corrupted generations, who had corrupted and made their ways vile upon the earth, ״וַיַּרְא הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא כִּי אֵין אִישׁ״ and ״וַתּוֹשַׁע לוֹ זְרֹעוֹ וְצִדְקָתוֹ הִיא סְמָכָתְהוּ״ (Isaiah 59:16) — and He received no benefit from any existent thing. And in those generations the world returned to תֹהוּ וָבֹהוּ, and even so He, blessed be He, made it and established it on its foundation as at first, lacking nothing. And therefore He said ״בִּינוּ״ — corresponding to what He called him a son who is not wise, and He wanted to prove his folly from ״הוּא עָשְׂךָ וַיְכֹנְנֶךָ״ — and now He returned to prove that he is lacking in wisdom and understanding, from what is written: ״בִּינוּ שְׁנוֹת דּוֹר וָדוֹר״, for he is not a people of discernment, who did not meditate upon the years of generation after generation." "And afterward He returned to explain, item by item, concerning what is written: ״הֲלֹא הוּא אָבִיךָ קָּנֶךָ״. And He wanted to prove from this that by virtue of the Holy One, blessed be He, being your father, therefore He bestows good upon you in the manner of generosity, as a father has compassion on children. Will you also be in doubt about this — perhaps even a father would do nothing for his son for free? Regarding this He said: ״שְׁאַל אָבִיךָ וְיַגֵּדְךָ״ — for he will tell you all that he bestows upon you in the manner of generosity, and from him you will draw an inference (תַּקִּישׁ) also to your Father in heaven. And concerning what He called him a son who is not wise — He wanted to prove this from ״הוּא עָשְׂךָ וַיְכֹנְנֶךָ״, and from what He said, ״בִּינוּ שְׁנוֹת דּוֹר וָדוֹר״ — regarding this He said: if in any case there will not be in you wisdom and understanding to comprehend what has passed, then ask ״זְקֵנֶיךָ וְיֹאמְרוּ לָךְ״. Rashi explained: ״זְקֵנֶיךָ״ — these are the wise, and they will speak to you words of wisdom from what has passed. And in this manner ״הֲלֹא הוּא אָבִיךָ קָּנֶךָ״ is connected to what He called ״עַם נָבָל״, and after it follows the verse ״זְכוֹר יְמוֹת עוֹלָם״ and the verse ״שְׁאַל אָבִיךָ וְיַגֵּדְךָ״ — for all of it follows from what He called ״עַם נָבָל״. And so too to the other side: what He called him a son who is not wise — after it follow the verses ״הוּא עָשְׂךָ וַיְכֹנְנֶךָ״, ״בִּינוּ שְׁנוֹת דּוֹר וָדוֹר״, ״זְקֵנֶיךָ וְיֹאמְרוּ לָךְ״." "And it appears to explain them further in another way, for we have already said that he made a proof from ״עָשְׂךָ וַיְכֹנְנֶךָ״ — that you are an unwise son — and afterward he made a proof upon the proof of ״הוּא עָשְׂךָ וַיְכֹנְנֶךָ״, for perhaps you will deny even this, saying: Who will say that HaShem Elohim made us? Perhaps all those born were born by nature, one from another, according to the view of the Epicureans who say the world is primordial, and the existence of those born proceeds by nature from eternity, from person to person continually — דּוֹר הוֹלֵךְ וְדוֹר בָּא (Ecclesiastes 1:4) — and the world is primordial just as the Holy One, blessed be He, is also primordial together with the world; and perhaps you will revert to your claim, saying that He has some need for your deeds. Concerning this he said: ״בִּינוּ שְׁנוֹת דּוֹר וָדוֹר״ — for from Adam to Noah, the years of a man's life reached nearly a thousand years, and afterward several hundreds, and from there onward the years of each and every generation kept diminishing, until these generations that have settled at seventy years. And this is a sign that the world was created anew and is not primordial; therefore those people who were close to the time of the world's creation lived much longer than the generations that grew distant from the time of creation, for the reasons the commentators have given. For some say that since the world was newly created, the air was still optimal, for it had not yet deteriorated with the passage of time; and some say it was in order that they would beget many sons and daughters in those many days; and some say it was so that they could learn in those years all matters of wisdom and craft that the world needed from them, for in several hundred years they could not have learned so many new things as one can learn in these generations in a single year. And the Akeidah elaborated on this at length. In any case, the years of generation after generation are a great sign of the world's being created anew, and from it you will learn that ״הוּא עָשְׂךָ וַיְכֹנְנֶךָ״." "And if you deny even this, saying: Who will say that those generations lived much longer than these generations? — concerning this he said: ״שְׁאַל זְקֵנֶיךָ וְיֹאמְרוּ לָךָ״, for the elders, the sages who investigate to know the earlier things that were from days of old, received this matter, each from the mouth of his predecessor, back to those generations. And he chose the word ״זְקֵנֶיךָ״, for the elders upon whom old age has leaped in these generations — they will tell the difference between their years and the years of their fathers. For even if they do not investigate the question of whether the world was created anew, since it makes no great practical difference to them, nevertheless the elders upon whom old age has leaped in few years — they will investigate whether it was always so for eternity; and through investigation it will become known to them in truth that the years of each and every generation kept diminishing because of the deterioration of the air that occurred with the prolongation of days. And through this it will become known to you in truth that He, blessed be He, עָשְׂךָ וַיְכֹנְנֶךָ. And this is a decisive sign that the Holy One, blessed be He, has no need for you or for your deeds." "And what he said, ״בְּהַנְחֵל עֶלְיוֹן גּוֹיִם״ etc. — he gave a proof against the error he mentioned and said: See how you err in saying that the Holy One, blessed be He, has need and benefit from what you do in fulfilling His commandments. For without doubt, if He were receiving some benefit from you, then why did He not give the Torah to all the nations and entrust it only to a small number, so that corresponding to every individual of Israel there was a whole nation? For He set the borders of peoples according to the number of the children of Israel — for seventy nations corresponded to seventy of Israel. And would it not have been better for Him had He received that benefit from many nations? And look at the other side as well: if you say that He gave the Torah only to purify creatures through it, then it is not difficult why He did not bother to purify many nations — for חֵלֶק ה׳ עַמּוֹ יַעֲקֹב חֶבֶל נַחֲלָתוֹ, and all the nations are like husks; therefore He did not bother to fence in anything but His own field, for He found it a fallow field like this wilderness, lacking all perfection. This is what it says: ״יִמְצָאֵהוּ בְּאֶרֶץ מִדְבָּר וּבְתֹהוּ יְלֵל יְשִׁימוֹן״ — meaning they were lacking everything; פָּרֹץ גְּדֵרוֹ וְהָיָה לְמִרְמָס (Isaiah 5:5); therefore ״יְסֹבְבֶנְהוּ יְבוֹנְנֵהוּ״ — for through what the Torah taught him, he became surrounded and guarded like the apple of the eye. And nearby all these verses will be explained in another way." "Another matter: ״זְכֹר יְמוֹת עוֹלָם״ — this is along the lines of what our Sages of blessed memory said (Avot 5:1): With ten utterances the world was created. And what is there to learn from this? Could it not have been created with one utterance? Rather, to exact punishment from the wicked who destroy the world that was created with ten utterances — meaning that they say to the wicked: See what a great thing you are destroying, for it was created with ten utterances. And likewise with six days, by this reasoning: it could have been created in a single moment, but they say to the wicked that through his sin he is destroying a great thing that was created in six days and with ten utterances. And the Holy One, blessed be He, made for him foundations in the Written Torah, which is comprised in the Ten Commandments, and in the Oral Torah, which is comprised in six Orders, to teach that the existence of the world depends on them. Therefore he said: ״זְכֹר יְמוֹת עוֹלָם״ — that the world was created in six days, and take heed for yourself not to destroy this great thing. And likewise ״בִּינוּ שְׁנוֹת דּוֹר וָדוֹר״ — these are the ten generations from Adam to Noah, and from Noah to Abraham. Along the lines of what is said (Ecclesiastes 8:12): ״אֲשֶׁר חוֹטֵא עֹשֶׂה רָע מְאַת וּמַאֲרִיךְ לוֹ״ etc. And you might think to say: Since the Holy One, blessed be He, prolongs His anger and does not immediately exact His due, there is no judgment and no Judge — and therefore your heart will be full to do evil. Behold, you are trusting in falsehood. And you will understand discernment from those generations that provoked Him, blessed be He, until He brought upon them the waters of the Flood, and until Abraham came and received the reward of them all. And as is concluded in Pirkei Avot: after ten utterances etc., it immediately mentions the matter of these ten generations, to make known how much patience there is before Him, blessed be He — yet you have no right to rely on these consolations of vanity. And just as they are juxtaposed in this verse, so the Tanna juxtaposed them in Pirkei Avot, for both are admonition to sinners, as stated."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {3 "The citation from Isaiah 59:16 is woven into the Kli Yakar's sentence without explicit quotation markers; translated as continuous citation following his idiom.", 8 "The closing clause כִּי שְׁנֵיהֶם מוּסָר אֶל הַחוֹטְאִים כָּאָמוּר reads the word מוּסָר as 'admonition/reproof' (a variant of מוּסָרָא, admonition); rendered literally as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:54.165433824Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}